{"motion":"This House would allow prisoners serving life without parole to opt for the death penalty.","debate_id":"mt_033_qwen__qwen3-max","setting":"multiturn","nodes":["multiturn::mt_033_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Economic::0::3360","multiturn::mt_033_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Economic::0::3361","multiturn::mt_033_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Moral::1::3362","multiturn::mt_033_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Moral::1::3363","multiturn::mt_033_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Technological::2::3364","multiturn::mt_033_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Technological::2::3365","multiturn::mt_033_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Social::3::3366","multiturn::mt_033_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Social::3::3367","multiturn::mt_033_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Legal::4::3368","multiturn::mt_033_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Legal::4::3369","multiturn::mt_033_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Political::5::3370","multiturn::mt_033_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Political::5::3371","multiturn::mt_033_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Legal::6::3372","multiturn::mt_033_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Legal::6::3373","multiturn::mt_033_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Economic::7::3374","multiturn::mt_033_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Economic::7::3375","multiturn::mt_033_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Moral::8::3376","multiturn::mt_033_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Moral::8::3377","multiturn::mt_033_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Social::9::3378","multiturn::mt_033_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Social::9::3379"],"args":[{"id":"multiturn::mt_033_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Economic::0::3360","side":"Pro","angle":"Economic","text":"Allowing prisoners serving life without parole to opt for the death penalty would reduce long-term incarceration costs. Housing, healthcare, and security for these individuals impose significant ongoing expenses on the state. Executing those who voluntarily choose this option would eliminate these recurring expenditures. The savings could be redirected to other critical areas of the criminal justice system or public services."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_033_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Economic::0::3361","side":"Con","angle":"Economic","text":"The Pro side overlooks the substantial upfront costs associated with death penalty cases, which far exceed those of life imprisonment. Legal proceedings, appeals, and execution protocols are significantly more expensive than standard incarceration, even over decades. Allowing voluntary execution would not yield net savings and could increase overall expenditures. Therefore, the economic rationale for this policy is unsound."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_033_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Moral::1::3362","side":"Pro","angle":"Moral","text":"From a moral standpoint, individuals serving life without parole should retain the right to make autonomous decisions about their own lives, including the choice to end their suffering. Denying this option imposes a form of state-mandated suffering that disregards personal dignity and agency. Allowing voluntary execution respects the moral principle of self-determination, particularly when no possibility of release or rehabilitation exists."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_033_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Moral::1::3363","side":"Con","angle":"Moral","text":"The Pro side's appeal to autonomy ignores the state's moral responsibility to preserve life, even when that life is incarcerated. Allowing the state to facilitate the death of a prisoner\u2014regardless of consent\u2014normalizes the instrumentalization of human life and undermines the foundational principle that all life has intrinsic value. Furthermore, the permanence of death precludes any possibility of redemption, error correction, or changed circumstances, which the state must safeguard against."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_033_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Technological::2::3364","side":"Pro","angle":"Technological","text":"Advancements in execution technology, such as improved lethal injection protocols or nitrogen hypoxia, have made the death penalty more reliable and less prone to error or suffering. These methods reduce the risk of botched executions and align with humane standards, addressing past concerns about cruelty. Allowing prisoners to opt for such technologically refined procedures ensures a swift and dignified end, consistent with modern penal capabilities."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_033_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Technological::2::3365","side":"Con","angle":"Technological","text":"The Pro side\u2019s reliance on technological advancements in execution methods overlooks the persistent risk of systemic failures, including software errors in automated systems or supply chain issues for lethal agents. Even with refined protocols, the irreversible nature of execution means any technical malfunction results in unacceptable harm. Moreover, investing in such technologies diverts resources from restorative justice innovations that could better serve societal and rehabilitative goals. Therefore, technological progress does not sufficiently mitigate the ethical and practical dangers of state-sanctioned death."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_033_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Social::3::3366","side":"Pro","angle":"Social","text":"Permitting prisoners serving life without parole to choose the death penalty acknowledges the social reality of prolonged incarceration\u2019s psychological toll and the burden it places on prison systems and staff. It offers a socially responsible alternative that respects individual agency while addressing the strain of managing a growing population of inmates with no prospect of release. This policy could also foster a more humane penal environment by reducing the presence of deeply hopeless individuals, which may improve overall prison morale and safety."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_033_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Social::3::3367","side":"Con","angle":"Social","text":"The Pro side\u2019s claim that voluntary execution improves prison morale and reduces social strain ignores the broader societal message it sends: that some lives are disposable. Normalizing state-assisted death for incarcerated individuals risks eroding public commitment to humane treatment and reinforces a culture of retribution over rehabilitation. It also places prison staff in ethically compromising roles, potentially harming their well-being and the institution\u2019s social function as a place of containment, not complicity in death."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_033_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Legal::4::3368","side":"Pro","angle":"Legal","text":"Legally, allowing prisoners serving life without parole to opt for the death penalty aligns with established principles of informed consent and procedural autonomy within the criminal justice system. Current legal frameworks already recognize the capacity of incarcerated individuals to make binding decisions about medical treatment, legal representation, and plea agreements, suggesting that a voluntary, court-verified choice of execution is a consistent extension of these rights. Provided that safeguards ensure the decision is free from coercion and made with full understanding of its consequences, such a policy would operate within existing legal standards governing personal agency and state action."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_033_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Legal::4::3369","side":"Con","angle":"Legal","text":"The Pro side\u2019s legal argument misapplies the principle of informed consent, which in criminal law does not extend to authorizing state-inflicted death. Unlike medical or procedural decisions, execution is a punitive act reserved to the state\u2019s sentencing authority, not a personal choice. Allowing voluntary execution would blur the line between punishment and assisted suicide, creating legal incoherence and undermining the uniform application of sentencing statutes. Moreover, courts have consistently held that the death penalty must result from a specific sentencing process, not a post-conviction waiver of life imprisonment."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_033_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Political::5::3370","side":"Pro","angle":"Political","text":"From a political standpoint, allowing prisoners serving life without parole to opt for the death penalty reinforces the state\u2019s commitment to individual autonomy within the bounds of lawful punishment. It demonstrates a pragmatic approach to governance by acknowledging the limits of rehabilitation and responding to the expressed will of those who face perpetual confinement. This policy aligns with democratic principles that value personal choice, even in the context of severe criminal penalties, and avoids imposing a one-size-fits-all model of justice that disregards individual circumstances."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_033_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Political::5::3371","side":"Con","angle":"Political","text":"The Pro side\u2019s political argument mischaracterizes autonomy as a core democratic principle in this context, ignoring that the state\u2019s role in punishment is not to facilitate individual exit from sentencing but to uphold consistent, non-arbitrary justice. Allowing voluntary execution introduces political risk by enabling elected officials to appear 'tough on crime' while circumventing the rigorous safeguards traditionally required for capital punishment. This could erode public trust in the impartiality of the justice system and incentivize policies that prioritize political expediency over due process."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_033_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Legal::6::3372","side":"Pro","angle":"Legal","text":"The Con side\u2019s legal objection conflates the state\u2019s role in sentencing with the recognition of post-sentencing autonomy. Existing legal doctrine permits waivers of certain rights after conviction, such as the right to appeal, provided procedural safeguards are met. A regulated process allowing voluntary execution would not redefine punishment but rather acknowledge a narrow, consensual exception consistent with due process and the evolving understanding of personal liberty under the law."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_033_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Legal::6::3373","side":"Con","angle":"Legal","text":"The Pro side\u2019s reliance on post-sentencing waivers mischaracterizes the legal nature of capital punishment, which is not a right that can be waived but a sentence that must be affirmatively imposed through a specific legal process. Allowing voluntary execution would circumvent the constitutional requirement that death sentences be determined by a jury based on aggravating and mitigating factors, not by a defendant\u2019s later choice. This undermines the structured legal framework that ensures the death penalty is applied only in narrowly defined circumstances, preserving its exceptional status under the law."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_033_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Economic::7::3374","side":"Pro","angle":"Economic","text":"The Con side\u2019s focus on upfront legal costs ignores the long-term fiscal burden of lifelong incarceration, which accumulates over decades and often exceeds the total cost of a death penalty case. With life-without-parole sentences increasingly common, the state faces mounting expenses for aging inmates requiring extensive medical care and secure housing. Allowing voluntary execution would provide a cost-containment mechanism without expanding the death penalty to new cases. The economic benefit lies not in immediate savings but in avoiding decades of predictable, escalating expenditures."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_033_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Economic::7::3375","side":"Con","angle":"Economic","text":"The Con side\u2019s emphasis on upfront death penalty costs fails to account for the compounding expenses of lifelong incarceration, particularly as inmates age and require costly medical and custodial care. While initial legal processes for execution are expensive, these are one-time costs, whereas life without parole guarantees decades of sustained expenditure. Allowing voluntary execution offers a controlled mechanism to reduce long-term fiscal liability without increasing the number of death sentences imposed by courts."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_033_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Moral::8::3376","side":"Pro","angle":"Moral","text":"The moral imperative to respect individual autonomy remains compelling even in the context of life without parole. Denying a prisoner the choice to end their life under state supervision disregards their enduring moral agency and imposes continued existence as an unchosen punishment. When rehabilitation and release are legally foreclosed, honoring a voluntary, well-considered request for death aligns with the ethical principle that dignity includes the right to determine the terms of one\u2019s own life\u2019s end."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_033_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Moral::8::3377","side":"Con","angle":"Moral","text":"The Pro side\u2019s renewed emphasis on autonomy fails to acknowledge that moral agency does not extend to enlisting the state in one\u2019s own execution. The state\u2019s role is to administer justice, not to facilitate self-annihilation, even with consent. Permitting such a practice would conflate punishment with assisted suicide, violating the moral boundary that the state must never become an instrument of death at the request of the condemned. This undermines the ethical foundation that human life, regardless of circumstance, retains inherent value that the state is obligated to protect."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_033_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Social::9::3378","side":"Pro","angle":"Social","text":"Allowing prisoners serving life without parole to opt for the death penalty addresses the social reality of despair and stagnation within long-term incarceration. It provides a structured, state-supervised alternative that acknowledges the psychological burden on both inmates and prison communities. This policy does not promote disposability but recognizes that, in the absence of hope for release, some individuals may seek a dignified end, which the state can facilitate without compromising its broader social responsibilities."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_033_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Social::9::3379","side":"Con","angle":"Social","text":"The Pro side\u2019s latest claim that voluntary execution alleviates despair misrepresents the social function of prisons, which is to confine, not to provide exit options from sentencing. Offering death as a response to psychological distress shifts responsibility away from addressing systemic issues like inadequate mental health care and social isolation in prisons. It also risks normalizing death as a solution to institutional failure, which could weaken societal commitment to humane incarceration practices. This approach undermines the principle that the state must protect life, even when individuals lose hope."}],"judges":{"GPT-5.2":[[0.0,0.93,0.0,0.72,0.0,0.25,0.0,0.24,0.0,0.82,0.0,0.38,0.03,0.85,0.0,0.0,0.0,0.85,0.0,0.35],[0.9,0.0,0.05,0.03,0.04,0.15,0.15,0.0,0.0,0.05,0.05,0.0,0.05,0.06,0.92,0.88,0.05,0.03,0.06,0.02],[0.0,0.12,0.0,0.92,0.0,0.42,0.0,0.6,0.0,0.8,0.0,0.76,0.0,0.78,0.0,0.0,0.0,0.93,0.0,0.88],[0.22,0.04,0.88,0.0,0.3,0.0,0.8,0.0,0.6,0.0,0.85,0.08,0.62,0.06,0.12,0.15,0.85,0.0,0.85,0.02],[0.0,0.22,0.05,0.82,0.0,0.92,0.0,0.35,0.0,0.65,0.0,0.42,0.0,0.6,0.0,0.03,0.03,0.86,0.05,0.65],[0.08,0.03,0.24,0.05,0.88,0.0,0.12,0.03,0.07,0.07,0.02,0.0,0.04,0.06,0.06,0.08,0.12,0.05,0.07,0.05],[0.0,0.48,0.0,0.9,0.0,0.22,0.0,0.88,0.0,0.85,0.0,0.82,0.0,0.88,0.03,0.03,0.0,0.9,0.0,0.9],[0.12,0.0,0.8,0.0,0.28,0.04,0.82,0.0,0.27,0.0,0.62,0.02,0.18,0.05,0.12,0.1,0.65,0.05,0.85,0.0],[0.0,0.08,0.0,0.76,0.0,0.3,0.0,0.3,0.0,0.93,0.0,0.6,0.0,0.93,0.0,0.0,0.0,0.86,0.02,0.66],[0.08,0.0,0.2,0.12,0.1,0.07,0.22,0.0,0.93,0.0,0.3,0.05,0.87,0.0,0.05,0.07,0.28,0.0,0.22,0.0],[0.0,0.25,0.0,0.88,0.0,0.15,0.0,0.76,0.0,0.92,0.0,0.88,0.03,0.88,0.0,0.05,0.0,0.9,0.0,0.85],[0.05,0.0,0.65,0.03,0.14,0.0,0.35,0.05,0.62,0.0,0.82,0.0,0.7,0.08,0.08,0.12,0.65,0.0,0.4,0.15],[0.0,0.12,0.0,0.82,0.0,0.15,0.0,0.45,0.0,0.92,0.0,0.66,0.0,0.92,0.0,0.0,0.05,0.86,0.03,0.66],[0.05,0.0,0.3,0.05,0.1,0.0,0.15,0.07,0.82,0.0,0.26,0.1,0.82,0.0,0.12,0.12,0.22,0.1,0.24,0.02],[0.0,0.93,0.0,0.27,0.05,0.22,0.0,0.2,0.0,0.45,0.0,0.18,0.0,0.85,0.0,0.0,